1. c4 g6 2. Nc3 Bg7 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. d4 h6 8. e4
Kh7 9. h3 Ng8 10. Be3 e5 11. Qd2 Qe7 12. Rad1 exd4 13. Bxd4 f6 14. Nd5 Qd8 15.
b3 Ne5 16. Ne1 Nc6 17. Bc3 Be6 18. f4 f5 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. Qc3+ Kh7 {
"Forgetting that the rooks were disconnected, [Golombek] went} 21. e5 dxe5 22.
Nb6 $4 {"and resigned on the 29th move." (BCM, Aug 1935, p352)} Qxd1 23. Nxa8
Nd4 24. Kh1 Rxa8 25. fxe5 Rd8 26. Nd3 Ne2 27. Rxd1 Nxc3 28. Rd2 Ne4 {Source:
The Times, 20 Jul 1935. Golombek in 'Chess Treasury of the Air' (1966) by
Terence Tiller: "In the penultimate* round I met Harold Saunders, a player
whom I had beaten resoundingly on the three occasions when we had met. Now I
chose to make a nice combination against him involving a piece sacrifice and
overlooking that I had a pawn on d5 that blocked the long diagonal of my g2
bishop. That cost me the title*." (* incorrect on two counts - the game
occurred in the last round and had Golombek won, he would still have been half
a point behind Winter)} 0-1

