21. Rh8+ ({"Here White had the game in his hands by playing} 21. Qh4 {at once;
but he thought he saw a mate in 6 or 7 moves (barring 2 checks from Black) by
the sacrifice of both of his rooks! So the game continued...}) 21... Kg7 22.
R1h7+ Nxh7 23. Rxh7+ Kg8 24. Rh8+ Kg7 ({Now if} 24... Kxh8 25. Qh4+ Kg7 26.
Qh7+ Kf8 27. Qh8# {. However, Black played 24...Kg7 and there followed...}) 25.
Qh4 {Threatening mate on the move!} Qxb2+ 26. Nd2 $4 (26. Ke1 $18) 26... Bg4+
$3 {White must now take the bishop...} 27. Qxg4 {...whereupon Black played}
Rxh8 {and was left practically with a won game. On your secretary [Lacy
Hulbert] telling him so however, he very chivalrously insisted on resigning,
saying that he had touched a piece on his second move and not moved it! He had
intended resigning all along, but had only waited to see if he could win! Mr
Hulbert appealed to the controller to annul the resignation, but he was
powerless! Source: Kington Times, 20 July 1935.} 1-0

